#import "/shared/book.typ": *
#let p = problem.with(code: "MM7033", unit: "U8")
#let items = (
 p(1,"E","U8.1 Noetherian examples",[Explain why every finite-dimensional vector space is a Noetherian module over its field.],[An increasing chain cannot increase dimension indefinitely.],[Equal-dimensional nested subspaces are equal.],[The dimensions in an increasing chain are nondecreasing integers bounded by the space's finite dimension. They eventually stabilize, and nested subspaces of equal finite dimension are equal. Thus the chain stabilizes.],[Justify equality once dimensions stop increasing.]),
 p(2,"E","U8.1 Hilbert's theorem",[Why is $K[x,y,z]$ Noetherian for a field $K$?],[A field has only two ideals.],[Apply Hilbert's theorem three times.],[A field is Noetherian because its ideals are zero and the whole field. Hilbert's theorem successively gives Noetherianity of $K[x]$, then $K[x,y]$, then $K[x,y,z]$.],[Name the base case and each finite-variable extension.]),
 p(3,"E","U8.2 Coordinate rings",[Show $K[x,y]/(x y)$ is not a domain.],[Use the two coordinate classes.],[Neither class lies in the ideal, but their product does.],[The classes of $x$ and $y$ are nonzero: a multiple of $x y$ cannot equal either polynomial, as evaluation at $y=0$ or $x=0$ confirms. Their product is zero by the defining relation. Hence the quotient has zero divisors.],[Prove the two factors are nonzero.]),
 p(4,"E","U8.2 Specialization",[Compute $R^3 otimes_R R/M$ for a maximal ideal $M$.],[Tensor commutes with finite direct sums.],[Each copy of $R$ becomes $R/M$.],[The result is $(R/M)^3$, a three-dimensional vector space over the residue field. The standard basis tensors give its basis.],[Identify the coefficient field.]),
 p(5,"C","U8.1 Quotients",[Prove a quotient of a Noetherian module is Noetherian.],[Lift an increasing chain through the quotient map.],[The preimages form an increasing chain of submodules.],[An increasing chain of quotient submodules lifts to a chain of submodules containing the kernel. The original module is Noetherian, so the lifted chain stabilizes. Taking images under the quotient map shows the original quotient chain stabilizes.],[Use the submodule correspondence.]),
 p(6,"C","U8.1 Finitely generated submodules",[Prove the ascending chain condition implies every submodule is finitely generated.],[Assume a counterexample and choose generators one at a time.],[At each finite stage choose an element outside the current span.],[A non-finitely-generated submodule allows a choice $m_1$, then $m_2$ outside $R m_1$, then $m_3$ outside their span, and so on. The resulting finitely generated spans form a strictly increasing chain, contradicting the ascending chain condition. Thus every submodule is finitely generated.],[Explain why the process cannot terminate under the contrary assumption.]),
 p(7,"C","U8.1 Infinite polynomial ring",[Show $K[x_1,x_2,dots]$ is not Noetherian.],[Use ideals generated by initial segments of variables.],[The next variable does not belong to the preceding ideal.],[The chain $(x_1) subset(x_1,x_2) subset dots$ is strictly increasing. Indeed setting the first $n$ variables to zero kills the $n$th ideal but does not kill $x_(n+1)$. Thus the chain never stabilizes.],[Prove strictness at every stage.]),
 p(8,"C","U8.2 Local coordinate calculation",[Compute the localization at $x$ of $A=K[x,y]/(x y)$.],[Inverting $x$ forces $y$ to vanish.],[The remaining coordinate is an invertible $x$.],[The localization is $K[x,x^(-1)]$. A map sends the class of $x$ to $x$, its inverse to $x^(-1)$, and $y$ to zero. Conversely the invertible class of $x$ defines a map from the Laurent polynomial ring into $A_x$. The composites fix all generators, including inverses, so are identities.],[Give both maps to rule out extra relations.]),
 p(9,"C","U8.2 Finite presentation",[Prove every finitely generated module over a Noetherian ring is finitely presented.],[Choose a finite free module surjecting onto it.],[Its kernel is finitely generated.],[Choose $R^n -> M$ onto. The finite free module is Noetherian, so its kernel has finitely many generators. A map from $R^m$ taking basis elements to those generators gives an exact presentation $R^m -> R^n -> M ->0$.],[Identify where Noetherianity is used.]),
 p(10,"A","U8.1 Localization preserves Noetherianity",[Prove a localization of a Noetherian ring is Noetherian.],[Contract an ideal of the localization back to the original ring.],[Every fraction in the ideal has its numerator in the contracted ideal after multiplying by a unit.],[Let $J$ be an ideal of $S^(-1)R$ and $I=\{a:a/1 in J\}$. Since $R$ is Noetherian, $I=(a_1,dots,a_n)$. If $a/s in J$, multiplying by $s/1$ gives $a/1 in J$, so $a in I$. Thus $a/s$ is a combination of $a_i/1$ with coefficients in the localization. Conversely these generators lie in $J$, so they generate it. Every ideal is finitely generated.],[Prove the contraction–extension equality used in the argument.]),
 p(11,"A","U8.2 Eigenspace decomposition",[Suppose $T^2=1$ on a vector space over a field of characteristic not two. Construct projections onto the two eigenspaces and prove a direct-sum decomposition.],[Use polynomials in $T$.],[Take $(1+T)/2$ and $(1-T)/2$.],[Let $P_+=(1+T)/2$ and $P_-=(1-T)/2$. Using $T^2=1$ gives $P_+^2=P_+$, $P_-^2=P_-$, $P_+P_-=0$, and $P_++P_-=1$. Also $T P_+=P_+$ and $T P_-=-P_-$. Hence their images lie in the two eigenspaces and every vector is their sum. The intersection is zero because the projections are orthogonal, giving the direct sum.],[The inverse of two is essential; state the characteristic hypothesis.]),
 p(12,"A","U8.1 Hilbert reduction step",[In the proof of Hilbert's theorem, explain in detail why finitely many polynomials with leading coefficients generating the leading-coefficient ideal suffice together with finitely many bounded-degree polynomials.],[Subtract shifted multiples to cancel the leading term.],[The bounded-degree intersection is a submodule of a finite free module.],[Let $f_i$ have degrees $d_i$ and put $N=max d_i$. For any ideal element of degree $d>=N$, express its leading coefficient as $sum r_i "lc"(f_i)$ and subtract $sum r_i x^(d-d_i)f_i$ to lower the degree. Repeating terminates below $N$. The ideal's intersection with polynomials of degree less than $N$ is finitely generated as an $R$-module because $R^N$ is Noetherian. Those generators finish the reduction.],[Explain termination and why coefficient generation alone does not handle low degrees.]),
)
